        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
              What is a Pomodoro Timer and how does it work?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2 leading-relaxed">
              A <strong>Pomodoro Timer</strong> is a time management tool that breaks
              work into intervals, traditionally 25 minutes in length, separated by
              short breaks. These intervals are named pomodoros. The timer tracks
              these cycles to encourage sustained focus and prevent mental fatigue.
              Users work during the focus round, then take a short break, and repeat
              the process.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
              Is Pomodoro 20 or 25 minutes?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2 leading-relaxed">
              The traditional Pomodoro interval is 25 minutes of focused work
              followed by a 5-minute break. However, the best interval is the
              one that fits your personal focus span. Our customizable <strong
                >aesthetic pomodoro timer</strong
              > allows you to edit and set custom durations (such as 20, 25, or 50
              minutes) to perfectly match your cognitive rhythm.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
              Is 25/5 or 50/10 Pomodoro better?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2 leading-relaxed">
              Neither is objectively better; it depends on the nature of your
              tasks. A 25/5 interval (25 minutes work, 5 minutes break) is
              excellent for administrative tasks, writing, or getting started
              when motivation is low. A 50/10 interval (50 minutes work, 10
              minutes break) is better for deep work like coding, designing, or
              complex reading where getting into a flow state takes longer.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
              What are the 5 steps in the Pomodoro Technique?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2 leading-relaxed">
              The five core steps of the Pomodoro Technique are: (1) Choose a
              task you want to get done. (2) Set the <strong
                >pomodoro timer</strong
              > to 25 minutes (or your custom duration). (3) Work on the task until
              the timer rings. (4) Take a short break (typically 5 minutes). (5) After
              every four Pomodoros, take a longer break (typically 15-30 minutes)
              to recharge.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
              How many Pomodoros is 1 hour?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2 leading-relaxed">
              In 1 hour (60 minutes), you can complete two standard 25-minute
              Pomodoros (50 minutes total work time) plus two 5-minute breaks
              (10 minutes total break time). If you adjust your timer to a
              custom duration, the number of cycles will vary.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
              Is Pomodoro only for ADHD?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2 leading-relaxed">
              No, the Pomodoro Technique is beneficial for everyone, but it is
              highly recommended for individuals with ADHD or neurodivergent
              profiles. The visual countdown, structured breaks, and division of
              tasks into manageable chunks help reduce executive dysfunction,
              alleviate overwhelm, and make starting tasks much easier.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink text-sm">
              How do I customize the Focus and Break durations on this Pomodoro
              Timer?
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-mute mt-2 leading-relaxed">
              To customize focus intervals and break durations, switch to the
              Pomodoro Timer mode, click the edit gear icon next to the mode
              buttons, and enter your preferred times (up to 180 minutes for
              focus, 60 minutes for short breaks, and 120 minutes for long
              breaks). Click "Apply Changes" to update your timer. The
              application will warn you if a timer is active and will save your
              custom durations automatically to local storage so they persist on
              your next visit.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
